# Line endings
#
# Everything textual is LF in the working tree, on every platform. The inherited
# file said only `* text=auto`, which leaves the working tree up to the client:
# with the Git for Windows default (core.autocrlf=true) the whole tree checks
# out as CRLF. The generators in tools/ read in text mode -- so they would still
# agree -- but they *write* '\n', which the Python text layer turns into '\r\n'
# on Windows. Every generated file would come back modified with no real change,
# and `ctest -R ui_forms` would be comparing CRLF against LF.
#
# `eol=lf` settles this in the repository instead of in each developer's config.
# See docs/PLAN-WINDOWS.md, task T1.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for what the generators read and write, so that a future edit
# to the line above cannot silently take these with it. The .sh files are the
# ones that break outright rather than noisily: bash reads the shebang and every
# line after it verbatim, and CRLF gives `$'\r': command not found`.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.cpp   text eol=lf
*.hpp   text eol=lf
*.h     text eol=lf
*.ui    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.rc    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.qrc   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf

# Binary. Git's heuristic already gets these right; saying so means `text=auto`
# never has to guess, and no diff is attempted.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.bin   binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary

# The author's own debug dumps, committed as they were produced: CRLF, and not
# ours to renormalise. Nothing reads them -- they are history, not fixtures.
docs/samples/debugio.txt        -text
docs/samples/debugio_robben.txt -text

# Diff drivers inherited from the original .gitattributes.
*.cs     diff=csharp
*.doc    diff=astextplain
*.DOC    diff=astextplain
*.docx   diff=astextplain
*.DOCX   diff=astextplain
*.dot    diff=astextplain
*.DOT    diff=astextplain
*.pdf    diff=astextplain
*.PDF    diff=astextplain
*.rtf    diff=astextplain
*.RTF    diff=astextplain
